{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"400\tnext time; the recorded `size` is that line's byte offset, not the file's, or\n401\tthe next sweep would see no growth and strand the very line held back. The\n402\tcondition is deliberately narrow (unterminated file *and* final line): a parse\n403\tfailure anywhere else is real corruption, and stopping there would stall the\n404\tsession's capture forever. An *unsettled record* — an opencode tool part still\n405\t`running`, a Gemini message whose tool calls have no result yet — does the\n406\tsame, so the finished version is captured once, in final form, rather than\n407\tfrozen half-done into an immutable event id.\n408\t\n409\t## Privacy and integrity\n410\t\n411\tVisible tool output can contain secrets. The trust boundary is transport:\n412\tnative transcripts already sit in plaintext on the capturing machine, so\n413\tredaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. But once a\n414\tsecret reaches a note, removal is expensive (the notes ref's own history\n415\tretains prior blobs), so prevention layers run at capture and the last\n416\tcheckpoint runs at sync. Content hashes (event ids) make accidental\n417\tmutation detectable.\n418\t\n419\t### Redaction layers\n420\t\n421\tDefense in depth, ordered by where they run and what they may do:\n422\t\n423\t- **A. Capture-time pattern redaction (default on).** A deliberately\n424\t  conservative, versioned ruleset of unambiguous secret formats — prefixed\n425\t  API tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`,\n426\t  `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private-key blocks, JWTs — applied to every\n427\t  draft inside `appendEvents`, before ids are computed. Matches in both\n428\t  `content` and `raw.data` are replaced with a deterministic placeholder\n429\t  (see below). No entropy heuristics here: a false positive silently\n430\t  rewrites the record, which violates the preservation commitment, so\n431\t  capture-tier rules must be near-zero-false-positive by construction.\n432\t- **B. External scanners as test oracle only.** gitleaks/trufflehog-class\n433\t  rulesets are used in the test suite to prove the capture tier catches\n434\t  what it claims (secret corpus in, zero findings out) — never vendored\n435\t  into the runtime path.\n436\t- **C. Env-value masking (opt-in, default off).** Scrub exact values of\n437\t  local environment variables / `.env` entries from captured content.\n438\t  Highest recall for unstructured secrets, but `.env` is often plain\n439\t  config, and the transform depends on machine state — so it is\n440\t  nondeterministic across rescans (id churn is the documented cost of\n441\t  opting in).\n442\t- **D. User pattern rules (config).** Extra redaction regexes merged into\n443\t  the capture tier from config. Path-based exclusion (\"never record reads\n444\t  of `secrets/**`\") is planned; it requires correlating `tool_use` inputs\n445\t  with `tool_result` events and is not in v1.\n446\t- **E. Sync-time scan (default on, tiered).** Before push, scan only the\n447\t  events the remote does not yet have. The default profile runs\n448\t  medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run for events captured\n449\t  under older rules, keyword-anchored assignments, URL credentials); on a\n450\t  hit the push blocks with an interactive finding report — allowlist the\n451\t  false positive (persisted by fingerprint) or `cledger redact` the real\n452\t  secret while purge is still a local operation. Entropy heuristics live\n453\t  behind an opt-in paranoid tier; `--no-scan`/config disables the gate\n454\t  entirely. Scan-tier rules may be noisy precisely because they only warn\n455\t  a human — they never rewrite anything.\n456\t\n457\t  **Reports carry coordinates, never content** (0.16.0). Findings print\n458\t  event id, rule, JSON path + offset, and fingerprint; no excerpt. Masking\n459\t  only the matched span was not enough: the *context* around a match is what\n460\t  tripped the rule in the first place — a `keyword-assignment` excerpt\n461\t  necessarily reprints the keyword that anchored it — so a report captured\n462\t  into a later conversation re-seeded a finding on the report itself, and\n463\t  each scan compounded the last. Observed while dogfooding turnbridge: one\n464\t  discussion of GitHub Actions code multiplied into repeat findings that\n465\t  `cledger allow` could not durably clear, because every new report minted a\n466\t  new event to flag. An event id and a JSON path match no secret rule, so\n467\t  they stay safe to reprint indefinitely.\n468\t\n469\t  Readable output moved to `cledger inspect <event-id>`, which renders wide\n470\t  surrounding context (default 400 chars each side, `--context N`), masks the\n471\t  match unless `--reveal`, writes to a mode-0600 file outside the repo rather\n472\t  than stdout, and **refuses to run inside a coding-agent session** (detected\n473\t  via the harnesses' own env markers; `--force` overrides). That last guard is\n474\t  the load-bearing one: an agent asked to investigate a blocked sync will\n475\t  otherwise do the helpful, wrong thing and read the secret straight into the\n476\t  record being protected. Reports therefore address humans and agents\n477\t  separately, and tell the agent to stop rather than merely warning it.\n478\t- **F. Known-secret learning (opt-in, default off).** Runs at capture like\n479\t  A/C/D, but is *sourced* from the E flow: a `cledger redact --pattern`\n480\t  remembers the exact values it scrubbed in a local, git-invisible store\n481\t  (`.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`), and capture-time redaction\n482\t  exact-matches them out of every future draft under a `known-secret` rule id.\n483\t  This closes the capture side of the capture/scan feedback loop — a value E\n484\t  catches but A missed can never be re-captured raw once confirmed. Like C it\n485\t  is exact-value and therefore machine-dependent (id churn is the accepted\n486\t  cost); only `--pattern` feeds it (`--all` blanks whole content, no reusable\n487\t  value) and sub-8-char values are dropped to avoid over-matching. It stores\n488\t  plaintext by necessity (fingerprints are one-way and can't drive exact\n489\t  matching) but under `.git/` and written `0600`, exactly as local-and-unshared\n490\t  as the transcripts the values came from — consistent with the transport\n491\t  boundary. Off by default: the store is never read or created unless the flag\n492\t  is set. Note the interaction with the id-preservation rule below: once a\n493\t  value is remembered, re-capturing a source line containing it yields\n494\t  *scrubbed* content and therefore a different id, so it no longer dedups\n495\t  against the redacted original — a second scrubbed copy, not a resurrected\n496\t  secret. Id churn is the accepted cost, identical to C.\n497\t\n498\t### Redaction metadata\n499\t\n500\tA capture-time redaction replaces the matched span with\n501\t`[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]` — fingerprint is a truncated\n502\t`sha256(secret)`, letting identical secrets correlate across events without\n503\tbeing recoverable (brute-forceable only for low-entropy values, which the\n504\tcapture tier does not target). The event also carries a `redactions` array\n505\t(rule id, ruleset version, fingerprint, location path); it sits outside the\n506\tidentity subset, since the rewritten `content` already determines the id.\n507\tDeterminism rule: capture-tier redaction must be a pure function of source\n508\tcontent + versioned ruleset, or rescans duplicate events. Ruleset upgrades\n509\ttherefore change ids on forced rescan — same accepted caveat as identity\n510\tstamping; cursors prevent it in normal operation.\n511\t\n512\tA human redaction of an *existing* event (the E flow) instead rewrites the\n513\tnote line and appends a companion `redaction` event with `links.redacts`,\n514\tthen