{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"493:- **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n494-  repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n495-  fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n496-  like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n497-  a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n498-  secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n499-  it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n500-  cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n501-  tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n502-  no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n503-  recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n504-  not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n505-  should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n506-  allowlist for itself.\n507-  - **The false positive is self-perpetuating through documentation**\n508-    (observed 2026-07-27, from `intent-recall`): a design conversation that\n509-    quoted *this very roadmap entry* — the paragraph above, containing the\n510-    example `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")` — was itself flagged\n511-    with 6 `keyword-assignment` findings and held back from push. The\n512-    write-up of a false positive reproduces the false positive, in a\n513-    different repository, in a conversation transcript rather than in code.\n514-    Two things this exposes that the parent entry does not: (a) the allowlist\n515-    is per-repo, so a fingerprint retired here is re-encountered from scratch\n516-    by every downstream repo whose conversations read or discuss these docs —\n