{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11\tsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12\tride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13\tpre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14\t\n15\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16\t\n17\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20\t\n21\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t```sh\n27\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29\tcledger install all    # hook capture into every supported coding CLI\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`,\n33\ta `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`, and a\n34\t`session.idle` plugin at `~/.config/opencode/plugin/cledger.js`, backing each\n35\tup first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n36\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n37\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n38\tduplicates.\n39\t\n40\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n41\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n42\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n43\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n44\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently\n45\t(for opencode, which has no transcript file, use `cledger capture opencode\n46\t--all` or `--session <id>`; `gemini-cli` and `qwen-code` also accept `--all`,\n47\twhich backfills every session that CLI scopes to the current directory).\n48\t\n49\tThe hooks above are installed once, globally, and fire in every git\n50\trepository you work in. To turn cledger off for one specific repo, add\n51\t`{\"enabled\": false}` to that repo's `.cledger.json`: hook capture, manual\n52\t`cledger append`, and backfill all become no-ops, before any ledger read or\n53\twrite happens. Existing history is untouched and still readable via\n54\t`cledger log`/`show`/`export` — this only stops new events from being\n55\twritten. Outside any git repository, hooks are already a silent no-op, so\n56\tthere's nothing to record and nothing to disable.\n57\t\n58\t## Use\n59\t\n60\t```sh\n61\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n62\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n63\tcledger log --model gpt-5.6-sol # only turns a given model served\n64\tcledger log --with-state        # include session state, activity, injections, file snapshots\n65\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch, with the models they used\n66\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order (matches subagents too)\n67\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n68\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n69\tcledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n70\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\t## Sharing (transport)\n74\t\n75\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n76\t\n77\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n78\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n79\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n80\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n81\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n82\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n83\t- **Push is branch-scoped** (0.14.0): only conversations reachable from the\n84\t  refs you're pushing are shared, so an abandoned or unmerged branch's\n85\t  conversations stay on your machine. Squash-merged work still rides along —\n86\t  the scope follows `re_anchor` mappings the same way reads do.\n87\t  `cledger sync --all` pushes the whole ledger; `--rev R` scopes elsewhere.\n88\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n89\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n90\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n91\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n92\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n93\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n94\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n95\t  explicitly either way.\n96\t\n97\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n98\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n99\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n100\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n101\t\n102\t## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n103\t\n104\tEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n105\tmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n106\tbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n107\tthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n108\t\n109\t- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n110\t  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n111\t  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n112\t  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n113\t  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n114\t  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n115\t  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n116\t  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n117\t- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n118\t  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n119\t  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n120\t  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n121\t  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n122\t  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n123\t  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n124\t  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n125\t  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n126\t  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n127\t  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n128\t  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n129\t  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n130\t  turns them off.\n131\t- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n132\t  working either way.\n133\t\n134\t## What gets recorded\n135\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_018RRXq5NfpqwUTiqv3QXvYR","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"redaction-ux","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger-worktrees/redaction-ux","head":"fb4df15a7bdebc38ca45aa5ad908b0fcb411d13c","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:b8bc0200-5312-42a4-9c54-a116d8d4a925#agent:ac12a741cf47e6cde","parent":"claude-code:b8bc0200-5312-42a4-9c54-a116d8d4a925","seq":45},"id":"ev1-4d5fcc0602b4279e665fa2cb8025dced63472227cf7019f09bc5e7c832ac83c6","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-12T02:14:27.619Z","producer":{"session_id":"b8bc0200-5312-42a4-9c54-a116d8d4a925","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.228","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"ac12a741cf47e6cde","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger-worktrees/redaction-ux","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"redaction-ux","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11\tsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12\tride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13\tpre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14\t\n15\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16\t\n17\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20\t\n21\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t```sh\n27\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29\tcledger install all    # hook capture into every supported coding CLI\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`,\n33\ta `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`, and a\n34\t`session.idle` plugin at `~/.config/opencode/plugin/cledger.js`, backing each\n35\tup first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n36\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n37\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n38\tduplicates.\n39\t\n40\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n41\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n42\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n43\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n44\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently\n45\t(for opencode, which has no transcript file, use `cledger capture opencode\n46\t--all` or `--session <id>`; `gemini-cli` and `qwen-code` also accept `--all`,\n47\twhich backfills every session that CLI scopes to the current directory).\n48\t\n49\tThe hooks above are installed once, globally, and fire in every git\n50\trepository you work in. To turn cledger off for one specific repo, add\n51\t`{\"enabled\": false}` to that repo's `.cledger.json`: hook capture, manual\n52\t`cledger append`, and backfill all become no-ops, before any ledger read or\n53\twrite happens. Existing history is untouched and still readable via\n54\t`cledger log`/`show`/`export` — this only stops new events from being\n55\twritten. Outside any git repository, hooks are already a silent no-op, so\n56\tthere's nothing to record and nothing to disable.\n57\t\n58\t## Use\n59\t\n60\t```sh\n61\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n62\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n63\tcledger log --model gpt-5.6-sol # only turns a given model served\n64\tcledger log --with-state        # include session state, activity, injections, file snapshots\n65\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch, with the models they used\n66\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order (matches subagents too)\n67\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n68\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n69\tcledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n70\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\t## Sharing (transport)\n74\t\n75\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n76\t\n77\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n78\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n79\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n80\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n81\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n82\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n83\t- **Push is branch-scoped** (0.14.0): only conversations reachable from the\n84\t  refs you're pushing are shared, so an abandoned or unmerged branch's\n85\t  conversations stay on your machine. Squash-merged work still rides along —\n86\t  the scope follows `re_anchor` mappings the same way reads do.\n87\t  `cledger sync --all` pushes the whole ledger; `--rev R` scopes elsewhere.\n88\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n89\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n90\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n91\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n92\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n93\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n94\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n95\t  explicitly either way.\n96\t\n97\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n98\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n99\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n100\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n101\t\n102\t## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n103\t\n104\tEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n105\tmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n106\tbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n107\tthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n108\t\n109\t- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n110\t  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n111\t  exactly — same tree, or