{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"376\t  like normal operation.\n377\t- **`opencode export` truncates a piped stdout at 64KB** — an upstream Bun\n378\t  flush bug: the process exits without draining stdout, so any session over\n379\t  ~64KB comes back cut off, and the truncated JSON can still parse. cledger\n380\t  works around it by routing the child's stdout to a temp file rather than a\n381\t  pipe (see `runOpencode`). Worth reporting upstream; the workaround should\n382\t  stay regardless, since it costs nothing and protects older opencode\n383\t  versions.\n384\t- **Config sections replace rather than merge, and it fails open** — `loadConfig`\n385\t  merges `~/.config/cledger/config.json` and `<repo>/.cledger.json` per\n386\t  *top-level section*, not per key (`const redact = override.redact ??\n387\t  base.redact`). So a repo that commits a `.cledger.json` with *any* `redact`\n388\t  key — a `patterns` list, say — silently replaces the user's entire `redact`\n389\t  section, and a globally-enabled `redact.knownSecrets: true` reverts to its\n390\t  default of `false`. Capture-time scrubbing of learned secret values then\n391\t  stops in that repo, with nothing printed and nothing in the ledger to show\n392\t  it happened. Same shape as the pre-0.13.0 worktree bug: silent, fails open,\n393\t  visible only if you go looking. Fix is a deep merge within each section, or\n394\t  at minimum a warning when a repo config overrides a section the user config\n395\t  had already set. Worth doing together with a general sweep for this class.\n396\t\n397\t  **Self-referential failures are now this project's characteristic bug\n398\t  family**, and are worth a standing check rather than four independent\n399\t  fixes. cledger records the work of building cledger, so its own state,\n400\t  output, and documentation feed back into its behavior. Known members: the\n401\t  capture/scan redaction loop (a finding report re-seeding the finding it\n402\t  describes — closed in 0.6.1/0.7.0); the pre-0.13.0 worktree state split\n403\t  (0.13.0); this config section-replace; and the allowlist entry below, whose\n404\t  *write-up* of a false positive reproduces the false positive in every repo\n405\t  whose conversations quote it. Three of the four fail silently, and two fail\n406\t  open. A useful tripwire when adding anything that reads cledger's own\n407\t  state or prints cledger's own findings: ask what happens when that output\n408\t  is itself captured.\n409\t- **Don't print findings by default; print a pointer to them** — the cleanest\n410\t  structural break in the self-referential loop above. Today `cledger scan`\n411\t  and the sync gate print every finding inline, which is exactly the text that\n412\t  gets captured into the next conversation and re-seeds the finding. Masking\n413\t  the matched span (0.6.1) removed the secret characters but not the\n414\t  *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is what\n415\t  `keyword-assignment` trips on — so the loop is narrowed, not closed.\n416\t  Proposed: report only the count and how to inspect, with the audience split\n417\t  made explicit in the text — a human is told which command reveals the\n418\t  findings; an agent is told not to run it, because reading the findings into\n419\t  its context is what reproduces them, and to surface the count to its human\n420\t  instead. Naming the failure mode in the output is the point: an agent that\n421\t  understands *why* will comply, where a bare \"don't look\" invites a\n422\t  workaround. Pairs naturally with the inspection command in the design doc's\n423\t  open questions, which already has to write unmasked output to a file rather\n424\t  than stdout for the same reason. *Tension to resolve first:* CI reads those\n425\t  printed findings, and `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is\n426\t  the documented CI integration — so the default likely has to be\n427\t  terminal-aware, or CI has to opt in explicitly (`--report`), rather than\n428\t  simply going quiet for everyone.\n429\t- **Branch-scoped push** *(shipped, 0.14.0)* — reachability filtered *reads*\n430\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, `conversations`), but the notes ref is a single ref\n431\t  and a ref push sends all of it, so conversations from a branch that was\n432\t  abandoned and never merged still reached `origin` and landed on every\n433\t  collaborator's disk. Two scopes wearing one name, with push the odd one out:\n434\t  storage was already per-commit and reads already per-branch.\n435\t\n436\t  Push now carries only the anchors a read at that rev resolves to. Storage is\n437\t  unchanged (commit-anchored, one local ref, one shared remote ref) — only\n438\t  push learns the granularity storage already had. `cledger sync` scopes to\n439\t  the current branch by default, `--rev` scopes elsewhere, `--all` restores\n440\t  the whole-ledger push. **No mapping is stored anywhere:** reachability is\n441\t  recomputed at push time from the DAG, the same reason reads never stored it.\n442\t\n443\t  *Squash merges are the case that decides the implementation.* The scope\n444\t  reuses `resolveAnchors` — the same function reads use — rather than a plain\n445\t  `git rev-list`. A squash discards the source branch's commits, so its\n446\t  conversations sit on anchors unreachable from the target, and only the\n447\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the *surviving* commit) tie them\n448\t  back. A rev-list-only scope would push those mapping events while leaving\n449\t  behind the events they point at — dropping exactly what re-anchoring exists\n450\t  to save. Covered by a test that fails against a rev-list-only scope.\n451\t\n452\t  *The destructive shortcut, recorded because it is the natural first\n453\t  implementation.* A notes ref is a snapshot of the whole database, not an\n454\t  append log: its tip tree maps every annotated commit to its note, so\n455\t  whatever tree you push becomes the remote's entire notes database. Filtering\n456\t  your local ref down to one branch and pushing that still fast-forwards — and\n457\t  silently deletes every other branch's conversations from the remote's\n458\t  current view (they survive in the ref's history but vanish from `git notes\n459\t  show` and from any fresh clone). The scoped push therefore seeds a temp ref\n460\t  from the remote's tip and unions its scope *into* that, so the push strictly\n461\t  adds. Also covered by a test that fails against the filtered-tree version.\n462\t\n463\t  *Side benefit:* the pre-push scan gate now inspects only what the push would\n464\t  carry, so a finding in a branch that is not being pushed no longer blocks\n465\t  shipping unrelated work.\n466\t\n467\t  *Scope follows the refs actually being pushed* (0.15.0). The hook is fed\n468\t  git's pre-push stdin — one line per ref — so `git push origin feat` from\n469\t  another branch shares feat's conversations. 0.14.0 shipped with `</dev/null`\n470\t  and scoped by `HEAD`, which shared nothing in that case. Ref deletions are\n471\t  skipped (no tip to scope to), multiple refs union, and anything unusable\n472\t  falls back to `HEAD` rather than to the whole ledger. Because this changes\n473\t  the installed hook, `installHook` now replaces a stale cledger block in\n474\t  place instead of leaving any file containing the marker untouched — only the\n475\t  marked region is rewritten, so surrounding user content survives.\n476\t\n477\t  *Not addressed here:* the read default. `cledger export` still defaults to\n478\t  no reachability filter, and the fix belongs in `readEvents` rather than in\n479\t  one CLI command, since library consumers (intent-recall) never go through\n480\t  the CLI and would otherwise inherit the unscoped view. Separate entry below.\n481\t  Per-branch remote refs (`…-branches/<branch>` plus a wildcard fetch refspec)\n482\t  were considered and are *not* needed; they would only add fetch-side\n483\t  selectivity, letting a collaborator decline a branch's conversations\n484\t  entirely. Worth revisiting only if that is ever wanted.\n485\t- **Reads default to unscoped** — `readEvents` applies reachability only when\n486\t  the caller asks, and `cledger export` never asks, so an automated consumer\n487\t  reading the ledger directly gets every branch's conversations unless it\n488\t  knows to pass a rev. Now that push is scoped, this is the remaining half of\n489\t  the same mismatch. The default belongs in `readEvents` (with an explicit\n490\t  opt-out) rather than in `export`, precisely because the consumers that\n491\t  matter — intent-recall and anything else using the library — never go\n492\t  through the CLI at all.\n493\t- **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n494\t  repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n495\t  fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n496\t  like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n497\t  a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n498\t  secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n499\t  it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n500\t  cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n501\t  tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n502\t  no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n503\t  recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n504\t  not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n505\t  should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n506\t  allowlist for itself.\n507\t  - **The false positive is self-perpetuating through documentation**\n508\t    (observed 2026-07-27, from `intent-recall`): a design conversation that\n509\t    quot