ng does not disable redaction for\n533\tanything captured afterwards. Post-push purge — notes-ref history rewrite,\n534\tforce-push, coordinated collaborator re-fetch — is deliberately separate,\n535\tdeferred, destructive tooling; until it exists, rotating the credential is\n536\tthe honest remedy for anything that has left the machine.\n537\t\n538\tSevering the chain is not an object purge, and the output no longer implies\n539\totherwise. `update-ref` leaves reflog entries pointing at the discarded\n540\tcommits, so the pre-redaction blob survives in the local object store until\n541\t`git reflog expire` plus a pruning `gc`. That residue is local-only — git\n542\ttransfers neither reflogs nor unreachable objects — so it never reaches a\n543\tremote and sits squarely inside the \"trust boundary is transport\" model. The\n544\tcommand reports what it did, what remains, and the command that clears it,\n545\trather than running a repo-wide destructive `gc` on the user's behalf.\n546\t\n547\t## Versioning against harness format changes\n548\t\n549\tThree layers exist today:\n550\t\n551\t1. `schema` on every event (`conversation-ledger/v1`) versions the ledger's\n552\t   own envelope.\n553\t2. `raw.format` (`claude-code-jsonl/1`, `codex-rollout-jsonl/2`) versions\n554\t   each adapter's interpretation of its native format; it must be bumped\n555\t   whenever the mapping changes, allowing later reprocessing to know which\n556\t   parser produced an event. (codex `/2`: `agent_message` payloads convert,\n557\t   encrypted blocks omitted — `/1` dropped those lines entirely.)\n558\t3. The native payload inside `raw.data` retains the harness's own version","tool_use_id":"toolu_01F3ovnqyJ4xw38Y7GvLNFud","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"60d636fe-128f-4dae-b69c-2601cf694d16","promptId":"b6bcbf20-f377-4512-9762-3e53a49290ec","sessionId":"b8bc0200-5312-42a4-9c54-a116d8d4a925","sessionKind":"bg","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"60d636fe-128f-4dae-b69c-2601cf694d16","timestamp":"2026-08-12T02:14:27.097Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"## Privacy and integrity\n\nVisible tool output can contain secrets. The trust boundary is transport:\nnative transcripts already sit in plaintext on the capturing machine, so\nredaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. But once a\nsecret reaches a note, removal is expensive (the notes ref's own history\nretains prior blobs), so prevention layers run at capture and the last\ncheckpoint runs at sync. Content hashes (event ids) make accidental\nmutation detectable.\n\n### Redaction layers\n\nDefense in depth, ordered by where they run and what they may do:\n\n- **A. Capture-time pattern redaction (default on).** A deliberately\n  conservative, versioned ruleset of unambiguous secret formats — prefixed\n  API tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`,\n  `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private-key blocks, JWTs — applied to every\n  draft inside `appendEvents`, before ids are computed. Matches in both\n  `content` and `raw.data` are replaced with a deterministic placeholder\n  (see below). No entropy heuristics here: a false positive silently\n  rewrites the record, which violates the preservation commitment, so\n  capture-tier rules must be near-zero-false-positive by construction.\n- **B. External scanners as test oracle only.** gitleaks/trufflehog-class\n  rulesets are used in the test suite to prove the capture tier catches\n  what it claims (secret corpus in, zero findings out) — never vendored\n  into the runtime path.\n- **C. Env-value masking (opt-in, default off).** Scrub exact values of\n  local environment variables / `.env` entries from captured content.\n  Highest recall for unstructured secrets, but `.env` is often plain\n  config, and the transform depends on machine state — so it is\n  nondeterministic across rescans (id churn is the documented cost of\n  opting in).\n- **D. User pattern rules (config).** Extra redaction regexes merged into\n  the