{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"536\t  refuses outright, before touching anything, when the target event is on\n537\t  `origin` — or when `origin` cannot be reached to rule it out (a security\n538\t  decision made on a failed network call must fail closed; previously an\n539\t  offline `ls-remote` was indistinguishable from a virgin remote). Gating is\n540\t  per *event*, not per repo, so an earlier push does not disable redaction\n541\t  for everything captured afterwards.\n542\t  Output no longer claims `history squashed: yes`. Severing the notes chain\n543\t  is real but is not an object purge — the old blobs remain in the local\n544\t  reflog and object store until git's expiry + `gc` — so the command now\n545\t  states exactly that, plus the fact that the residue is local-only (git\n546\t  transfers neither reflogs nor unreachable objects) and the one-liner that\n547\t  clears it. Running a repo-wide destructive `gc` on the user's behalf stays\n548\t  out of scope.\n549\t- **Reads must never prefer a pre-redaction copy** *(shipped)* — `dedupById`\n550\t  collapses same-id copies by earliest `recorded_at`, then by a canonical-JSON\n551\t  byte compare. A redacted event and its original share an id *by design*\n552\t  (the rewrite pins it, so a rescan dedups instead of resurrecting the\n553\t  secret) and, because the rewrite spreads the original, share `recorded_at`\n554\t  too — so both keys tied and the byte compare decided, resolving on the\n555\t  secret's first character against the `[` (0x5B) opening `[REDACTED:…]`.\n556\t  Every secret starting with a digit or an uppercase letter sorted below it\n557\t  and won, so reads printed the plaintext next to the `redaction` event\n558\t  asserting its removal. Redaction depth is now consulted first: the copy\n559\t  carrying more redaction records wins outright. Unambiguous, because\n560\t  capture-time redaction runs before ids are computed, leaving `redactEvent`\n561\t  as the only source of same-id different-content pairs.\n562\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n563\t  shared (notes-ref history rewrite + force-push + collaborator re-fetch\n564\t  coordination). This is now the *only* path for already-shared content,\n565\t  since `cledger redact` refuses it rather than pretending. Three hard parts\n566\t  scoped so far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator\n567\t  still holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so\n568\t  purge must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction\n569\t  and detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n570\t  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n571\t  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n572\t  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n573\t  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n574\t  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n575\t  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n576\t- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n577\t  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n578\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n579\t  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n580\t  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n581\t  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n582\t  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n583\t  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n584\t  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n585\t  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n586\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n587\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n588\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n589\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n590\t  scope and capture triggers.\n591\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n592\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n593\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n594\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n595\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n596\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n597\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n598\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n599\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n600\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n601\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n602\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n603\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n604\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n605\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n606\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n607\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n608\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n609\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n610\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n611\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n612\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n613\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n614\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n615\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n616\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n617\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n618\t  intended narrow form.\n619\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n620\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n621\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n622\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n623\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk — and it means `cledger redact --pattern`\n624\t  currently *increases* the number of plaintext copies of a secret on local\n625\t  disk, which is a surprising thing for a redaction command to do. Owner-only\n626\t  `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1.\n627\t  **Preferred fix: store hashes, not plaintext** (see the entry below) — it\n628\t  removes the secret rather than protecting it, and needs no key at all.\n629\t  The previously-planned alternative was encryption with an OS-keychain-held\n630\t  key (macOS Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt\n631\t  *non-interactively* since\n632\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n633\t- **Store hashes of known secrets, not the secrets** — replace\n634\t  `known-secrets.json`'s plaintext `values` with salted digests plus each\n635\t  value's byte length. Capture-time scrubbing then confirms a candidate span\n636\t  by hashing it, so the store stops being a readable aggregation of every\n637\t  secret you have ever confirmed. Strictly better than encrypting it: an\n638\t  encrypted store is reversible by design (capture must decrypt silently, so\n639\t  the key has to be reachable by anything running as you), whereas a hashed\n640\t  store does not contain the secret at all — not for an attacker, and not for\n641\t  cledger. It also deletes the whole key-management problem, which is the\n642\t  reason the encryption entry above has never been done.\n643\t  *The design question is candidate generation* — you cannot search for a\n644\t  value you cannot reconstruct, so something must propose the spans to hash.\n645\t  Two options, and they compose:\n646\t  (a) **Rule-generated candidates.** Run the broad scan-tier rules purely as\n647\t  candidate *generators* and confirm each match against the stored digests.\n648\t  Cheap — O(matches × stored) — and, importantly, the noise that disqualified\n649\t  those rules from capture-time *rewriting* is harmless here, because the\n650\t  hash comparison is exact: a false-positive candidate simply fails to match\n651\t  and nothing is rewritten. Its limit is recall, and the limit bites exactly\n652\t  where this feature earns its keep: a value the store holds *because no rule\n653\t  recognized it* still generates no candidate, so it is never checked.\n654\t  (b) **Length-indexed rolling scan.** Index the digests by length and sweep\n655\t  each string with a rolling hash (Rabin–Karp), running sha256 only on a\n656\t  window whose rolling hash hits. Linear in text size per distinct stored\n657\t  length, no dependency on any rule matching, and it subsumes (a) entirely.\n658\t  More code; the honest cost.\n659\t  *Accepted limitation either way:* a digest is brute-forceable for\n660\t  low-entropy values, so human-chosen passwords remain dictionary-attackable\n661\t  while random API tokens do not. That trade is deliberate — this tool\n662\t  operates on code repositories, where the realistic secret is a generated\n663\t  token, and a leaked credential's real remedy is rotation regardless. Salt\n664\t  per-store so the file is not rainbow-table-able, and note that storing\n665\t  lengths leaks 